--TEST--
`Markup + Markup` is `Markup`, so trusted fragments compose without a second decision — ADR 0024 § 5
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5: composing trusted fragments is what templating already does, so it
// stays cheap and stays trusted. Neither operand is escaped on the way in —
// re-escaping a fragment that was already lifted is what would corrupt it, and
// the `&` here is the character that would show it.
var $open = "<b>" as Core\Html\Markup;
var $body = "bold & wide" as Core\Html\Markup;
var $close = "</b>" as Core\Html\Markup;
echo $open + $body + $close, "\n";

// The result is a `Markup` and not merely bytes that print the same: the
// declared type is what asserts it, and composing the result a second time is
// what would stop compiling if `+` answered a `string`.
Core\Html\Markup $whole = $open + $body + $close;
echo $whole + $whole, "\n";

// A `Markup` is a value, peer to `string` the way `bytes` is: `+` builds a
// third fragment rather than growing the left one, so both operands read back
// exactly as they were written.
echo $open, "|", $close, "\n";
--EXPECT--
<b>bold & wide</b>
<b>bold & wide</b><b>bold & wide</b>
<b>|</b>
